  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-22",
    "title": "Swirls and Colors on Jupiter from Juno",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  What creates the colors in Jupiter's clouds? No one is sure. The thick atmosphere of  Jupiter  is mostly  hydrogen  and  helium , elements which are colorless at the low temperatures of the  Jovian cloud tops . Which trace molecules provide the colors remains a  topic of research , although small amounts of  ammonium hydrosulfide  are  one leading candidate . What is clear from the  featured color-enhanced image  -- and many similar images -- is that lighter clouds are typically higher up than darker ones. Pictured,  light clouds swirl  around  reddish regions  toward the lower right, while they appear to cover over some darker domains on the upper right. The featured image was taken by the robotic  Juno spacecraft  in 2018 during its  14th low pass over Jupiter .  Juno  continues in its looping elliptical orbit,  swooping near the huge planet  every 33 days and exploring a slightly different sector each time around.   Tomorrow's picture:  emerging darkness",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/JupiterSwirls_JunoBrealey_3709.jpg?w=3709&h=2772&fit=clip",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-22-swirls-and-colors-on-jupiter-from-juno/",
    "credit": " NASA ,  Juno ,  SwRI ,  MSSS ;  Processing &  License :   Matt Brealey ,  Seán Doran ",
    "alt": "A busy field of colorful clouds is pictured showing much interesting structure including many cloud swirls.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-22T09:19:15.551499+00:00"
